{# ── Chapter: Hook ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="hook">
  <h2>The agent deleted the right thing, in the wrong environment</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      You asked an agent to clean up old test data. It reasoned correctly about which rows were old test data, wrote a correct delete query, and ran it immediately, against production, because that's the database connection it happened to have open. Every individual step was right. The outcome wasn't.
    </p>
    <p>
      The problem was never the agent's reasoning. It was that "decide" and "execute" were the same step, with nothing in between where a human could have looked at the plan and said "wait, which database is this."
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Giving an agent tool access to production-affecting actions (deploys, deletes, config changes, sending real emails) without a review step before execution means every mistake in its plan becomes a mistake in production, instantly, with no chance to catch it in between.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      This isn't primarily an AI-safety framing, it's the same reason production deploys have had a review-then-execute step for humans for decades: the cost of an irreversible action is high enough that a cheap review gate is worth the friction, whether the plan came from a person or a model.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Concept ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="concept">
  <h2>Separate proposing an action from executing it</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Instead of the agent calling a tool and having it run immediately, the tool call becomes a proposal: here's exactly what I'm about to do, shown clearly to a human, with Approve and Reject buttons. Nothing happens until someone clicks Approve.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      The agent's tool-calling interface stays the same, what changes is what the tool actually does when called: instead of executing, it enqueues the action with its full parameters and returns a pending state. A separate execute step only fires after an explicit human approval event.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The gate needs to show the actual parameters the action will run with, not a paraphrase, a human approving "delete old test rows" without seeing the real <code>WHERE</code> clause is approving a summary, not the action, which defeats the point.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Story ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="story-1">
  <h2>The config change that shipped itself</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A team built an internal agent that could adjust feature-flag percentages based on error-rate monitoring, meant to slowly dial back a risky rollout automatically. It worked well in testing. In production, a monitoring blip made it read a temporary spike as sustained, and it dialed a completely unrelated flag to 0% for every user, instantly.
    </p>
    <p>
      Nobody had done anything wrong in the agent's logic reading THAT particular signal, the actual bug was subtle and took a while to find. What was avoidable immediately was the blast radius: the change went live the second the agent decided on it, with no step where a human on-call could have seen "wait, that's the wrong flag" before it took effect.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      The fix added an approval gate specifically for any flag change affecting more than 5% of users: the agent still proposed changes automatically and immediately, but execution required a human click, shown with the flag name, the before/after percentage, and the triggering metric, in a Slack message the on-call engineer could approve or reject in seconds.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      They deliberately didn't gate every action, small, easily-reversible flag nudges under 5% still executed automatically, since gating everything would have trained the on-call engineer to rubber-stamp approvals without really reading them. The gate's value depends on staying reserved for genuinely high-blast-radius actions.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

